<Base title="About This Site — Marcel Akiyama" description="Design references, generative art credits, and information about Marcel Akiyama’s personal website.">
 <header class="page-heading wrap"><p class="eyebrow">Colophon</p><h1>A small home<br/><em>for ongoing work.</em></h1></header><article class="prose narrow wrap"><section><h2>Design & generative art</h2><p>The design takes inspiration from the generous typography of <a href="https://dreamrecorder.ai/">Dream Recorder</a> and the editorial layouts of <a href="https://every.to/">Every</a>, including <a href="https://every.to/p/how-codex-happened">How Codex Happened</a>.</p><p>The geometric studies are original SVG code using radial symmetry, recursive branching, and overlapping ellipses. Creative-coding inspiration: Alex Postolache’s <a href="https://alexcodesart.com/drawing-a-mandala-with-p5-js-smooth-lines-speed-based-thickness-tweakpane-part-1/">mandala tutorial</a> and <a href="https://alexcodesart.com/drawing-fractals-with-l-systems-in-p5-js-a-creative-coding-tutorial/">fractal tutorial</a>. No tutorial code or artwork is reproduced.</p><p>Type is set in Instrument Serif and Inter, served from this site. Motion can be paused and respects reduced-motion preferences.</p></section><section><h2>Information & maintenance</h2><p>This is Marcel Akiyama’s first-person professional website. The biography describes professional experience; project pages distinguish experiments from established results and link to their source repositories.</p><p>AI assistance was used to build the site and the problem-framing course. Website copy, the plain-text profile, and the structured profile are generated from shared profile data to help keep them consistent.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/m5rc238/m5rc238.github.io">Website source ↗</a></p></section></article>
</Base>
